## sess129 — ruling blocker 4 landed on 0.11.445

Tree `0.11.444` → **0.11.445**, srcversion `AB4FA45D5B372180CE9E90C`, builds clean. Not deployed; fleet stays on 0.11.440. **Still STOP-SHIP** — blockers 2, 3 and blocker 7's surviving half remain.

I continued D-SAMENODE-WAITER-CANCEL-COLLISION (ledger #16) rather than starting at #1, same reason as sess119–128: the DLM core is mid-landing and sess126 set the order.

**Blocker 4 — the sweep re-claimed the same entry forever.** The bucket cursor is deleted and replaced by a real FIFO. The invariant is `queued IFF pending && !owed_busy`, maintained at four points that all already run under `lreq_lock`, and funnelled through one `lreq_oq_sync` so there is a single place to audit. It is a memory-safety property, not just fairness: `lreq_gc` already refuses on both halves of the right-hand side, so a queued entry can never be freed — and I added `oq_queued` as a gc refusal anyway, so weakening the invariant later leaks an entry (P248 already reports that) rather than corrupting the queue. The sweep is bounded by the queue length at start and rotates a declined entry to the tail; those two together are the whole anti-starvation argument. Releases re-enqueue at the tail so a repeatedly-failing entry can't re-occupy the head.

**Two findings that change the shape of blockers 2+3**, both line-verified:

Every obligation in the system is published from exactly **two** public entry points — `mxfs_dlm_caw_lock` and `mxfs_dlm_caw_convert`. The collector's own path through `caw_drop_own_waiter` takes `lreq_clr_begin_existing` and publishes nothing; the other two window sites pass a NULL intent. So "quiesce the producers" is an admission gate plus an in-flight counter on two functions, not a thread-by-thread argument — and the ruling's "if a BAST thread is both producer and transport, split those roles" doesn't arise, because the BAST threads aren't publishers.

Blocker 3's escalation mechanism already exists: `mxfs_dlm_shutdown_withdraw` → `schedule_work` → `mxfs_v5_dlm_shutdown_withdraw`, which fences new acquisitions, withdraws the disklock and unregisters the PR. A workqueue item is exactly the ruling's "context that cannot deadlock with `lreq_lock`". Only the wall-clock trigger is missing.

I also confirmed blocker 7 is the false positive sess126 suspected — the per-episode reset is genuinely there in `lreq_owed_retract`. Its surviving half (`owed_since_ms`) is blocker 3's wall-clock source, so it lands with blocker 3.

The teardown order has a real problem the next session has to move: the GOODBYE broadcast and `mxfs_disklock_release_slot` both run *before* the drain, so the node announces departure while its bits are still uncollected. The bdev close is correctly after.

Next: blockers 2+3 as one lifecycle change, with a RULE-5 consult first — it touches stop/mount and a force-shutdown path.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 29 open defects, 17 critical, DLM core under two stacked stop-ship rulings. Marker unwritten.

